  "summary": "Healthcare leaders are grappling with fundamental questions about AI's role in clinical care, with emerging consensus that artificial intelligence can accelerate research and discovery but cannot substitute for human clinical judgment in behavioral health and other specialties. Hartford HealthCare's development of a safer AI front door for patient interactions reflects broader industry caution around bias and reliability in clinical decision-support tools, where the complications of bias mitigation are receiving more nuanced attention than simple right-or-wrong framings allow. On the policy and economics front, discussions around Medicare Advantage's strategic direction, the WISeR model's viability, cost-reduction proposals from leaders like Paul Markovich, and coverage gaps for vulnerable populations highlight ongoing tension between market-based and safety-net approaches to healthcare access. Virtual care is gaining traction through transparency initiatives led by coalitions like DiMe, while international health IT innovation is being supported through HIMSS partnerships in Europe and anticipatory care models are reshaping how systems approach patient demand.",
